<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Arabisation</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">690s</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Arabisation refers to the policy, begun under the Umayyad <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> Abd al-Malik in the 690s, of making Arabic the official language of administration. Government registers previously kept in Greek or Persian were converted into Arabic, and a new Islamic coinage was introduced. The reform strengthened central control and the Arab and Islamic character of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/arabisation.jpg" alt="Arabisation" width="320" height="159" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Reinhard Saczewski · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Umayyaden-_Abd_al-Malik_-_M%C3%BCnzkabinett,_Berlin_-_5489972.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Arabisation</b> of the Christian population created a distinctive Mozarabic culture whose emigrants carried Andalusi styles into the northern Christian kingdoms.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Berber was the language of the court and of rural life, Arabic the language of law, government and most literature, and <b>Arabisation</b> advanced significantly.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Byzantine administrative framework in Syria, Palestine and Egypt was largely preserved, with Greek-speaking officials continuing to manage taxation, records and local government until Abd al-Malik's <b>arabisation</b> reforms of the late 690s made Arabic the language of the central registers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The region's Christians lived through a general decline under <b>Arabisation</b> and Islamisation, yet Syriac Christianity passed through a “Syriac Renaissance” in which discriminatory rules were lifted, especially after the death of the conservative Nur al-Din in 1174, and illustrated Gospels were made at Mar Mattai near Mosul around…</blockquote><a href="/empire/zengid-dynasty">Zengid dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its kinship with the conquerors’ language, also Semitic, probably eased the <b>Arabisation</b> of the Punic speakers.</blockquote><a href="/language/phoenician">Phoenician · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
